# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_NULL */
int64_t null(void);
/* CROI_SYS_TEST */
int64_t test(uint64_t op, ...);
```

# DESCRIPTION

`null` takes no arguments, touches nothing and returns 0.
It is the cost of entering and leaving the kernel; the null-call budget measures it.

`test` is for test boots.
It fails with `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED` unless the kernel was booted with `croi.debug_syscalls=on` or any `croi.test=` on its command line.
Its first argument <op> picks the operation; the others are that operation's.
It needs no handle.
It exists for the kernel's self-tests and the tests of user space, and an operation may reboot the machine, evict memory or sign with a kernel key, so a program has no use for it.
The operations are:

| op | Arguments | Does |
| 0 | | returns 1 if the calling thread's effective profile is a deadline one, else 0 |
| 1 | | binds the caller to the deadline context a kernel self-test provided (`CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` if none) |
| 2 | | unbinds it |
| 3, 4 | | returns the start or the deadline of the caller's current period |
| 5 | | returns the CPU number the caller runs on |
| 6 | count | runs <count> aging passes of the reclaimer |
| 7 | target, min age, ignore hint | evicts pages and returns how many |
| 8 | oom, critical, warning, hysteresis, delay | sets the memory watermarks, or with 0 restores the command line's |
| 9 | | returns the free memory in bytes |
| 10 | | returns the CPU of the context the caller borrowed, or -1 |
| 11 | value, modifier | arm64: returns the kernel key's pointer authentication signature of <value> |
| 12 | | arm64: returns 1 if guarded pages are in use |
| 13 | interrupt handle, time | fires the interrupt object at <time>, as its device would |
| 14 | value | hands <value> to the kernel's side of a self-test (`CROI_TEST_REPORT`) |

Any other <op> fails with `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.

